#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "5a63ca22-0b1a-5584-8b43-c80457016cd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7038" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=x^2$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ evaluate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + (x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049633}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561158 :question-generator/id #uuid "02fece60-f763-54e5-b044-cbd7cc18bd49" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186179674.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e57c7d-ef79-5439-be59-40bbdc9b3e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415412} {:answer/id #uuid "7cadedbb-d8e2-5bdf-97a6-9ad1eb1ceece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415420} {:answer/id #uuid "c58f6ab5-55eb-56af-a44a-0c359a236b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415424} {:answer/id #uuid "dc50983f-8d67-57c5-a859-8dc7f564511a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415427} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c20e31-578e-5df9-8b7f-fd828cbb1b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c20e31-578e-5df9-8b7f-fd828cbb1b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415429} :answer-field/id #uuid "55008b23-7b99-5d0d-87f9-a75bcea2d1c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93a8c36b-e0d3-5d9c-9527-8161a7655e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-39344" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t+2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =2+8 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22633649-e91e-5f15-bfbd-fcb4a483affb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348742} {:answer/id #uuid "3f792e82-8361-5835-a14b-ecd98c471f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349024} {:answer/id #uuid "9e65a7c0-308a-5d44-8737-c54996be3dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349860} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1e3e20-823b-5b23-90e3-b832e595b63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350119} {:answer/id #uuid "f37cb0c3-6210-59b3-8ea9-2f0e5f7a13b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f792e82-8361-5835-a14b-ecd98c471f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349024} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb520da-e760-5dc7-8812-5ec17ba0763e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2bdab86-46e0-5604-a07d-20d0ff29dcb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81178" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=(x+1)^{2}$ traversed from the point $P(0,1)$ to point $Q(1,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2(t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1+2(t+1)}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{t+1}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{1+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)]-[-\\frac{1}{0+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[-1+0] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fcc3d9-e48a-542e-9d30-6b397fb1a498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256961} {:answer/id #uuid "1869c282-abb4-524c-be28-650f45a1ebb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256994} {:answer/id #uuid "3697d3f0-d2a9-5157-9c3a-73b0ea60e2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257260} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0cfb60-19ef-5381-83bb-81e635e37ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257429} {:answer/id #uuid "b332b886-e893-52d6-b7df-0c964a1cd0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1869c282-abb4-524c-be28-650f45a1ebb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256994} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f64dde8-4609-5f96-b95a-2b0b8b027fbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbd34e81-f803-5fbc-b7c5-b362f720fe60" :question/math-academy-id "q-124664" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $P(1,2)$ to point $Q(4,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t({t}^{2}-2t)+({t}^{2}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2[(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+{1}^{2})] \\\\  & =2[4-\\frac{8}{3}+4-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}-1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{53}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181e2f70-d760-5c6f-a30f-585047479128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256990} {:answer/id #uuid "381edb78-eaaa-5872-8c1d-fd2478651f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257275} {:answer/id #uuid "55d0b7e0-71bf-594a-a93e-ca2764905f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257521} {:answer/id #uuid "71b1c76c-c17a-5ae5-a386-f4803282a9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257780} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9bb5b2-b51a-5f5b-9ec6-58471882be92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55d0b7e0-71bf-594a-a93e-ca2764905f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257521} :answer-field/id #uuid "039596a1-9fe5-54ad-8161-f20c3677fd50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "170a6ced-a411-5130-91d2-063d0a1b4a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-124668" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{3}$ traversed from the point $P(1,1)$ to point $Q(2,8),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e5bd11-b4a5-5c3f-97c8-baf947fdb648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324361} {:answer/id #uuid "ce09c054-3fce-534d-ab95-741a3083a7f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325760} {:answer/id #uuid "dc484259-e0ad-507c-9124-07e7bfbf313a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325892} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5488aa-cd87-5a5b-9265-c03f48ae0f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326037} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9c2e80-2a3e-55ba-bc7f-3b7949f67a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea5488aa-cd87-5a5b-9265-c03f48ae0f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326037} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f3e9a2-5330-5b08-8e51-b27a94666da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12ff35df-51f6-5e85-9109-59feb724214e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39679" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ traversed from the point $P(0,0)$ to point $Q(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t\\cdot {e}^{{t}^{3}\\cdot t}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{4}}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[3{e}^{{t}^{4}}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[3{e}^{(1)^{4}}+(1)]-[3{e}^{(0)^{4}}+(0)] \\\\  & =[3e+1]-[3] \\\\  & =3e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3db826-20ca-540b-9b96-6dc6ed206e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348865} {:answer/id #uuid "43f3642f-8a9e-5fee-a6d8-eabf284c8f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349065} {:answer/id #uuid "47e75774-fac8-5062-880a-727a16ba9eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349096} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecbe21c-5609-5fa1-ba0f-81eb95a13511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349608} {:answer/id #uuid "ae441ea5-b6f0-508a-83ed-e409f76a2a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae441ea5-b6f0-508a-83ed-e409f76a2a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349987} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf326210-69ad-557c-8a58-7a89edc90759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd7813dc-b09d-5c4d-8539-6d09e01430cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80983" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17becf89-3701-509a-a9a8-440484064ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348648} {:answer/id #uuid "20742a72-7769-5476-9ce9-3b1881e0470b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348727} {:answer/id #uuid "47810cbc-2cf1-5cf8-8896-58252baa012e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349094} {:answer/id #uuid "ee29d359-0faa-5f3d-aacd-2a4fd062f399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350529} {:answer/id #uuid "f83e5f36-bde0-573a-91de-bf6ecdf50c44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47810cbc-2cf1-5cf8-8896-58252baa012e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349094} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d0252a-6745-5c15-b97f-a22408b67a5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a071a2f-be85-5b88-9507-97c63159bbb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81098" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-x.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+(2t-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5480a9aa-ad9e-5901-99ef-a4b557010682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349219} {:answer/id #uuid "9515cda8-658c-596e-82bc-d1176ae74422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349782} {:answer/id #uuid "d708ba15-3640-5e0f-a2cc-8efebb555009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350330} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a9cd99-2cbe-59b5-8edc-4b78ef09dc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495} {:answer/id #uuid "f55e6f64-fa6a-5856-9b77-a53e676b505a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9a9cd99-2cbe-59b5-8edc-4b78ef09dc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed4da5c-67f9-5122-bb2a-f6a68757af78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bbe13972-64bc-5d76-9613-e21318b0221e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81180" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be1f2782-b94c-5338-8044-608f3468b85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356328} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6d80f7-dfb7-512a-abad-766ac81adffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356439} {:answer/id #uuid "d5012490-41ad-5072-9d60-53f86f6f37ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356527} {:answer/id #uuid "d7358768-6a5a-5876-8e36-c2f97c5a588a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356546} {:answer/id #uuid "e6036eac-887e-5728-bc69-99a208e6785a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6d80f7-dfb7-512a-abad-766ac81adffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356439} :answer-field/id #uuid "1281a916-cca9-5586-a7c5-f8adff0691eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d77bf889-9285-57c2-9d70-8b359a57d0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-90848" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1+1)\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{4}-5{t}^{2}+2{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}(2)^{3}+\\frac{{2}^{6}}{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =32-\\frac{40}{3}+\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a7777-a54c-5144-9dd2-6db2bdfadf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355029} {:answer/id #uuid "61232685-a7a4-54c0-b2f7-04ab7b7daab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} {:answer/id #uuid "9e198969-77e7-54c7-a4d5-8fcaa9d1d45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356073} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d4dda7-ea67-59f3-a16d-acadacf7c5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356127} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae3ce5-d578-5c1b-a2fb-4ead0dd1fc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61232685-a7a4-54c0-b2f7-04ab7b7daab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} :answer-field/id #uuid "331df704-6bf7-5c71-9966-44b1422cc8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "067b683a-f3df-5f66-b795-e612d7f9ac62" :question/math-academy-id "q-90871" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+4{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi {t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8\\pi {t}^{3}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =[2\\pi {t}^{4}-t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2\\pi (1)^{4}-1]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225888f9-79d1-51ec-b69e-df715e48a48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415403} {:answer/id #uuid "3341cdec-88e2-5336-a6c4-1be4742e22df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415408} {:answer/id #uuid "5b61e1f7-a8be-5897-ab62-f5700cfc5da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415414} {:answer/id #uuid "7872d0b7-7e4c-5866-a2c5-fa62a0852ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415418} {:answer/id #uuid "ff788f7f-efa0-5800-af8c-7f4c47429255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff788f7f-efa0-5800-af8c-7f4c47429255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac11d5c1-82f1-564e-be01-8e20a8e6d4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee6b1fc-a5dc-5685-b3c9-ad25e0e1efc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124666" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $P(1,0)$ to point $Q(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}\\cdot 1+t\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1+2{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}+2]-[\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}+2-\\frac{1}{5}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1262143b-7987-5866-b438-9f2860a6f4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544124} {:answer/id #uuid "1e590f80-d8cd-59b5-80b8-713187c9aae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544153} {:answer/id #uuid "7acbde24-d396-507e-bab6-65ef61f8c0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544358} {:answer/id #uuid "b92f50e1-a193-531b-b6de-1ecbee0c7393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544478} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6eaf80-e94b-55c5-8508-94c9b3e1a5d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b92f50e1-a193-531b-b6de-1ecbee0c7393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544478} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c8cb632-7694-5ff5-badc-7d0d79044a59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b91bd5f9-20da-5be7-a581-8dc4f819e202" :question/math-academy-id "q-90869" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}+1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+(y-1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(y-1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot 1+({t}^{3}+1-1)\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f68b7fd-a05b-5ed3-be29-d3a066fa3f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561103} {:answer/id #uuid "2da9c7fe-711b-5c16-9aaa-59dc3d066577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561107} {:answer/id #uuid "42241106-e146-5a54-bb14-23da41c8a163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561114} {:answer/id #uuid "7e85d298-fe5c-5353-8490-ad4ec7841c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561127} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd8ac6b-ee02-58b7-80d1-363a2c5eaef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbd8ac6b-ee02-58b7-80d1-363a2c5eaef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561140} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f2c8c9-f5fe-5b76-ad69-56e4496a7e41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "264b814d-2106-5f9b-b1fe-b44e3bc8871e" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}2 t+16 t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}16 t^{3} + 2 t\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[t^{2}+4 t^{4}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\left(1\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(1\\right)^{4}]-[\\left(0\\right)^{2}+4 \\left(0\\right)^{4}] \\\\  &=[1+4]-[0+0] \\\\  &=5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35fb8445-5684-548d-bd47-edcfcdeafbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561109} {:answer/id #uuid "41afe2ef-3ccf-5c85-b5c8-683beaddc0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561113} {:answer/id #uuid "759da95f-a53d-5bde-ae51-3f153ba102f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561124} {:answer/id #uuid "b14b3c92-fd7d-50b3-8c75-2db22af020f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561137} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb11971-a604-52b5-836e-e4a5989d12c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41afe2ef-3ccf-5c85-b5c8-683beaddc0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561113} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a10e453-cbdd-592f-963c-b2f496e834bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dcc6cc7-b54e-5741-ab6c-e8408719e9ef" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,3),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t^{2}+18 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}21 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[7 t^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[7 \\left(1\\right)^{3}]-[7 \\left(0\\right)^{3}] \\\\  &=[7]-[0] \\\\  &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae0dfcc4-e263-550a-a347-77b9c856215f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + 24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561135} {:answer/id #uuid "d81332bf-adb8-5841-99d3-9d72d8bfcb57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - 24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561145} {:answer/id #uuid "e66cffba-5fe7-572d-8b46-9c663c477ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561149} {:answer/id #uuid "e88587d0-0436-5fed-93df-1882ec786690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561150} {:answer/id #uuid "f931f17f-cdad-528e-9767-25b179cb2cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 24 \\pi - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0dfcc4-e263-550a-a347-77b9c856215f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + 24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561135} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f453c7f-8458-5c6f-9e7d-b329ad3c557e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c5590c4-444c-5199-8159-57617b0360a9" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 \\pi x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,12 \\pi),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,dy=$ The composed integrand at the initial endpoint is interpreted by its one-sided continuous extension along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=3 \\pi x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{\\left(3 \\pi t^{2}\\right)}{\\left(t\\right)^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(\\left(t\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 \\pi t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}-1+6 \\pi t^{3}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}6 \\pi t^{3} - 1\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[- t+\\frac{3 \\pi t^{4}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\  &=[- \\left(2\\right)+\\frac{3 \\pi \\left(2\\right)^{4}}{2}]-[- \\left(0\\right)+\\frac{3 \\pi \\left(0\\right)^{4}}{2}] \\\\  &=[-2+24 \\pi]-[0+0] \\\\  &=-2 + 24 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cc3c27-a174-5c0d-aa04-4811c96c7884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561118} {:answer/id #uuid "a9899e7a-d7ee-5995-9047-835d19bd680a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561134} {:answer/id #uuid "dd99d69a-966f-5260-8310-a3c9aa6518a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561147} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ce83be-6fda-55b0-a2e0-508f33e9fe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561151} {:answer/id #uuid "fff505a9-a05e-5262-8d36-577ee15f55fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff505a9-a05e-5262-8d36-577ee15f55fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561157} :answer-field/id #uuid "4db11ed0-4b57-51fe-90ac-743b3517303e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "578e10c6-79c2-549d-b8b1-370e0c81a999" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(2 x\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(3 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}3 t^{2}+4 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{1}7 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{7 t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\  &=[\\frac{7 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}]-[\\frac{7 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{7}{3}]-[0] \\\\  &=\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02aa9a57-14fb-5da2-89a8-c60019da0740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561102} {:answer/id #uuid "2b805d00-fa7e-59e2-bf53-fb6bd5ba499b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561106} {:answer/id #uuid "3f647e58-0307-54fb-91d2-41df1d42abcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561112} {:answer/id #uuid "779fd53c-1281-543d-924b-41becda0bec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561125} {:answer/id #uuid "93528aed-dc54-5dab-bbaa-d4d103cae7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{328}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b805d00-fa7e-59e2-bf53-fb6bd5ba499b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561106} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58510c5-ac6e-51f9-868d-1d0c4dc0f95a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7162142b-dbd1-534f-9062-4b9d8572ca7b" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2} - 3$ traversed from the point $A(0,-3)$ to point $B(2,1),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=x^{2} - 3.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2} - 3\\right)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\,dy &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(t\\right)^{2} \\left(t^{2} - 3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(3 \\left(t\\right)^{2} \\left(\\left(t^{2} - 3\\right) + 3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}t^{4} - 3 t^{2}+6 t^{5}\\,dt \\\\  &={\\int }_{0}^{2}6 t^{5} + t^{4} - 3 t^{2}\\,dt \\\\  &=\\left[\\frac{t^{5}}{5}- t^{3}+t^{6}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\  &=[\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{5}}{5}- \\left(2\\right)^{3}+\\left(2\\right)^{6}]-[\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{5}}{5}- \\left(0\\right)^{3}+\\left(0\\right)^{6}] \\\\  &=[\\frac{32}{5}-8+64]-[0+0+0] \\\\  &=\\frac{312}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27339d99-5bd9-5b84-ada5-cae5e808d7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561105} {:answer/id #uuid "8d49ffbc-81e9-5a34-8fee-73754515fc69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answe